  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "New benchmarks expose limited cross-source concordance and prior-dependent predictability, reframing how perturbation and drug-response models should be evaluated.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is auditing the data and priors that perturbation-response and drug-response models depend on, rather than proposing new architectures. A benchmark of six drug-perturbation transcriptomic resources finds limited cross-source concordance and identifies drug main-effect contributions. A gene-similarity framework comparing eight prior sources against Perturb-seq shows prior agreement tracks target-level predictability. New resources include four patient-derived organoid drug screens with multi-omics for training and benchmarking, and a community benchmark of 11 generative methods for synthetic bulk RNA-seq. Methodological work addresses confounding and disentanglement: a normalized HSIC kernel regularizer for single-cell counterfactuals and SCOUT for ATE/CATE estimation with noisy proxies. Together these emphasize that apparent predictive performance depends on dataset concordance, prior quality, and confounder handling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
